WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.711 --> 00:00:10.173
After suffering yet another night
with indigestion,

00:00:10.173 --> 00:00:15.053
George de Hevesy was convinced that
his kitchen at his boarding house

00:00:15.178 --> 00:00:20.558
was reusing and re-serving the
uneaten meat off diners’ plates.

00:00:21.184 --> 00:00:24.479
His landlady fiercely denied
the accusations.

00:00:24.896 --> 00:00:28.816
So, de Hevesy, who happened to be
a trained chemist,

00:00:28.858 --> 00:00:30.443
ran an experiment.

00:00:30.693 --> 00:00:35.740
He sprinkled a trace of radioactive
material onto his leftovers.

00:00:36.115 --> 00:00:41.704
Then, days later, he measured
the radiation levels in that night's hash,

00:00:41.954 --> 00:00:45.416
proving that his suspicions were correct.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:53.007
30 years later, de Hevesy was awarded
the 1943 Nobel Prize in Chemistry

00:00:53.007 --> 00:00:55.301
for developing radiotracing.

00:00:55.426 --> 00:00:59.764
The same technique he used to follow
leftovers through a kitchen

00:00:59.806 --> 00:01:02.683
could trace blood flow
after a heart attack,

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:08.898
drugs to their intended target, or even
groundwater through a watershed.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.654
The history of science is filled
with stories of self-experimentation.

00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:18.825
While some cases have resulted
in horrific infections,

00:01:18.908 --> 00:01:22.620
accidental psychedelic trips,
and even deaths,

00:01:22.829 --> 00:01:26.833
others have led to great leaps
in human understanding.

00:01:27.708 --> 00:01:31.546
But a few stories stand apart,
because like de Hevesy,

00:01:31.712 --> 00:01:37.385
their potentially dangerous acts
ultimately led to Nobel Prizes.

00:01:38.136 --> 00:01:44.225
In 1929, medical school graduate
Werner Forssmann had a radical idea

00:01:44.225 --> 00:01:47.353
for delivering lifesaving
drugs to the heart:

00:01:47.603 --> 00:01:52.900
insert a tube through the elbow
and thread it through the veins

00:01:53.025 --> 00:01:55.987
until it reaches the atrium directly.

00:01:56.195 --> 00:02:00.408
When his colleagues refused
to support a clinical trial,

00:02:00.575 --> 00:02:04.954
Forssmann decided to perform
the procedure on himself.

00:02:05.288 --> 00:02:07.748
Using only local anesthetic,

00:02:07.832 --> 00:02:10.877
he guided a catheter through his body,

00:02:11.043 --> 00:02:16.257
then walked downstairs for an X-ray
to confirm its position.

00:02:16.799 --> 00:02:19.635
He repeated the procedure
eight more times,

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:26.100
eventually using his thigh after his arm
vein closed from constant prodding.

00:02:26.475 --> 00:02:30.855
Nevertheless, his colleagues
saw these experiments as reckless,

00:02:30.938 --> 00:02:36.319
and the fallout forced Forssmann
to abandon cardiology altogether.

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:41.574
He’d go on to serve as a German
medical officer in World War II,

00:02:41.824 --> 00:02:44.994
and upon returning home in 1945,

00:02:45.244 --> 00:02:50.249
Forssmann was shocked to learn
that his heart catheterization procedure

00:02:50.333 --> 00:02:52.418
had become not just accepted,

00:02:52.585 --> 00:02:57.131
but essential for diagnosing
and treating heart conditions.

00:02:57.757 --> 00:03:02.011
This was thanks to André Cournand
and Dickinson Richards, who,

00:03:02.136 --> 00:03:06.390
in the intervening years,
had demonstrated the procedure’s benefits,

00:03:06.599 --> 00:03:09.268
using much more conventional methods.

00:03:09.769 --> 00:03:13.522
Two other laureates also
put their bodies on the line

00:03:13.648 --> 00:03:16.192
to prove the safety of their work.

00:03:16.317 --> 00:03:21.739
In the 1930s, virologist Max Theiler
dedicated his career

00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:23.699
to combating yellow fever,

00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:27.745
a highly dangerous mosquito-borne
viral disease.

00:03:28.037 --> 00:03:30.790
After developing an experimental vaccine,

00:03:30.957 --> 00:03:33.626
Theiler was one of the first
to receive it.

00:03:33.793 --> 00:03:38.839
Many vaccine researchers at the time
did this, even with the risks.

00:03:39.215 --> 00:03:44.762
Decades later, Chinese pharmacologist
Tu Youyou took on another killer:

00:03:44.971 --> 00:03:49.809
malaria, a parasitic infection
that invades red blood cells,

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:53.771
causing cycles of fever and severe anemia.

00:03:54.272 --> 00:03:57.275
Drawing from traditional
Chinese medical texts,

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.028
she found and isolated
the compound artemisinin,

00:04:01.237 --> 00:04:05.700
which appeared to kill the malaria
parasite in animal trials.

00:04:06.033 --> 00:04:11.664
To show it was safe and help move it into
testing with sick patients more quickly,

00:04:11.998 --> 00:04:14.208
Youyou took the first dose.

00:04:14.792 --> 00:04:18.504
But perhaps the boldest
of these laureates was the man

00:04:18.504 --> 00:04:22.300
who self-experimented not
to show something was safe,

00:04:22.425 --> 00:04:24.927
but to prove that it was dangerous.

00:04:25.344 --> 00:04:27.680
As early as the 1940s,

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.601
peptic ulcers, painful sores in the
stomach and small intestine,

00:04:32.727 --> 00:04:36.439
were believed to be primarily
caused by stress.

00:04:36.689 --> 00:04:42.236
But in 1979, Robin Warren and
Barry Marshall began noticing

00:04:42.236 --> 00:04:47.908
unusual spiral-shaped bacteria
in ulcer biopsy samples.

00:04:48.451 --> 00:04:52.997
They proposed that these bacteria
may be the true culprits.

00:04:53.414 --> 00:04:58.586
But after animal trials failed
to produce any convincing evidence,

00:04:58.586 --> 00:05:01.881
Marshall took matters
into his own... stomach.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.051
After confirming his gut was healthy,

00:05:05.051 --> 00:05:09.096
he drank a tube of the
spiral-shaped bacteria,

00:05:09.388 --> 00:05:12.433
later named Helicobacter pylori.

00:05:12.933 --> 00:05:18.064
Within five days, he developed
nausea, bloating, and stomach pain,

00:05:18.064 --> 00:05:23.361
and after 10 days, the bacteria had begun
damaging his stomach lining.

00:05:23.611 --> 00:05:26.530
Marshall treated himself with antibiotics,

00:05:26.697 --> 00:05:30.326
which became a standard
treatment for ulcers.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:36.165
We now know that if left untreated,
these ulcers can lead to cancer.

00:05:36.374 --> 00:05:42.713
So Marshall's decision to trust his gut
would go on to save countless lives.

00:05:42.797 --> 00:05:46.884
But his bold experiment,
along with those of other laureates,

00:05:47.009 --> 00:05:50.721
was based on years
of study and experience—

00:05:51.055 --> 00:05:54.600
not something just anyone
should try at home.
